Author a **cozy campfire**, a warm looping fire effect, as a 3D particle **system**
using only the particle tool.

It is a small campfire, already lit and burning steadily: licking flame tongues that
rise and flicker — yellow-white at the base, cooling to orange then red at the tips —
with small embers that pop upward and drift on the heat, and a faint smoke wisp
curling above. It is a **steady-state**, **seamlessly looping** effect: there is no
start or end, no ignition and no dying-out — the fire is always burning, and the loop
window flows continuously back into itself. You are authoring the *effect* (an
ongoing steady-state fire), not a single frame of it.

## How the particle tool works

The only way to shape the effect is the `particle-3d` binary already on your
`PATH`. It is the sole channel, and — this is the key idea — you do **not** place
individual particles. You **author a system**: emitters that spawn particles,
forces that push them around, and per-particle curves for size, opacity, and color
over each particle's life. The review UI **simulates that system live**, exactly the
way a real particle editor (Unreal's Niagara, Unity's VFX Graph) plays a system.
Because it is a live stochastic simulation, the effect **varies slightly from one
play to the next** — so build something that reads as a campfire across replays and
from any orbit angle, not a frozen arrangement.

You build the system by calling `particle-3d` **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered list of operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — is the
**authoritative output that is scored**. A system produced any other way is
discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
particle-3d add-emitter --name flame --shape disc --rate 40 --lifetime 700 --speed 5 --dir-y 1
```

This effect is a **steady-state loop**: keep the timeline looping so the fire is
always burning, and emit as a **continuous rate** (not a one-off burst) so the loop
window has no seam, no empty first frame, and no dying-out at the end. Rendering is a
separate, on-request step: run `particle-3d render` to **simulate the system over its
duration, write the preview animation, and emit the `system.json` your result is
built from**. An unrendered effect leaves an empty system, so you **must** run
`render` before you finish. The field dimensions, duration, and playback fps are
already seeded in a config next to your workspace, so no operation needs those flags.

Run `particle-3d --help` to list every operation and `particle-3d <operation>
--help` for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative
contract. **Read the rendered `effect.gif` after your calls** to judge the
effect's read, motion, and seamless loop, and decide what to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Author the campfire system operation by operation — the licking flame tongues, the
upward-drifting embers, and the thin smoke wisp (as continuous-rate emitters), with
the forces and per-particle curves that make the flames rise and flicker, the embers
drift on turbulence, and the smoke curl above, and the timeline kept **looping** so
the fire burns in steady state — running `particle-3d render` to simulate and check
the effect as you go. When the simulated `effect.gif` reads as a cozy campfire that
loops seamlessly with no seam and no burst-and-die, run `render` one last time and
stop: the recorded `actions.json` (and the `system.json` it emits) is your finished
submission.
